{"id":"reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named","date":"2026-10-21","topic":"code","region":"world","headline":"Why Linus Torvalds Named His Software Git","source":{"name":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Git"},"accent":"us","tiers":{"t1":{"sentences":[{"text":"Linus Torvalds made a new computer program in 2005.","voice":null,"roles":["S","S","V","O","O","O","O","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"He gave his work the short name Git.","voice":null,"roles":["S","V","O","O","O","O","O","O"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"In British English, a git means a bad person.","voice":null,"roles":["M","M","M","S","S","V","O","O","O"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"Torvalds laughed about the name of his program.","voice":null,"roles":["S","V","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"He said that he names everything after himself.","voice":null,"roles":["S","V","M","S","V","O","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null}],"bands":{"core1000":92,"core1800":0,"ngsl":0,"tsl":0,"nawl":0,"beyond":8},"grammar":[{"i":1,"focus":"gave his work the short name Git.","title":"Double Object Verb Pattern","note":"The verb 'give' can take two objects: the indirect object receiving something (his work) and the direct object being given (the short name Git). This structure shows who receives what without using a preposition like 'to'."},{"i":4,"focus":"names everything after himself","title":"Reflexive Pronouns","note":"Reflexive pronouns like 'himself' are used when the subject and object refer to the same person. Here, it shows that Linus names objects after his own persona."}]},"t2":{"sentences":[{"text":"Software builder Linus Torvalds created a computer system in 2005.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t2-0.m4a","roles":["S","S","S","S","V","O","O","O","M","M"],"dur":5.55,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.4,1.0,1.62,2.42,2.76,3.16,3.5,4.02,4.32]},{"text":"He named it Git, but this choice surprised many people.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t2-1.m4a","roles":["S","V","O","C","M","S","S","V","O","O"],"dur":3.76,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.06,0.26,0.48,1.5,1.74,1.94,2.22,2.66,3.1]},{"text":"The word git is British slang for a bad or foolish person.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t2-2.m4a","roles":["S","S","S","V","C","C","C","C","C","C","C","C"],"dur":4.64,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.08,0.3,0.84,1.42,1.78,2.16,2.58,2.76,3.14,3.42,3.88]},{"text":"People wondered about the strange name, so Torvalds answered with a joke.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t2-3.m4a","roles":["S","V","M","M","M","M","M","S","V","M","M","M"],"dur":4.55,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.24,0.52,0.92,1.08,1.34,2.48,2.58,3.26,3.64,3.92,4.0]},{"text":"He said that he names all of his projects after himself.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t2-4.m4a","roles":["S","V","M","S","V","O","O","O","O","M","M"],"dur":3.12,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.04,0.22,0.38,0.56,0.8,1.22,1.42,1.54,1.96,2.52]},{"text":"Today many developers use Git and they will use it for years.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t2-5.m4a","roles":["M","S","S","V","O","M","S","V","V","O","M","M"],"dur":4.67,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.2,0.78,1.34,1.88,2.02,2.68,2.8,2.96,3.18,3.34,3.56]}],"bands":{"core1000":88,"core1800":5,"ngsl":0,"tsl":2,"nawl":0,"beyond":5},"grammar":[{"i":1,"focus":"named it Git","title":"Object Complement Pattern","note":"Verbs of naming or calling take an object ('it') followed by a noun complement ('Git'). The complement defines what the object is called."},{"i":5,"focus":"will use it for years","title":"Future Tense with Will","note":"The auxiliary verb 'will' forms the simple future tense to express a prediction about future events. It indicates that developers' use of Git will continue over a long period."}]},"t3":{"sentences":[{"text":"In 2005, developer Linus Torvalds created Git, which is a tool that tracks code changes.","voice":null,"roles":["M","M","S","S","S","V","O","S","V","C","C","S","V","O","O"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"He needed a fast tool to build Linux, a famous operating system for computers.","voice":null,"roles":["S","V","O","O","O","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"However, the unusual name of the software surprised many people in the technology world.","voice":null,"roles":["M","S","S","S","S","S","S","V","O","O","M","M","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"In British English slang, a git is an annoying or unpleasant person.","voice":null,"roles":["M","M","M","M","S","S","V","C","C","C","C","C"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"When people asked about the title, Torvalds offered a funny explanation for his choice.","voice":null,"roles":["M","S","V","M","M","M","S","V","O","O","O","O","O","O"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"He joked that he has named all his major software projects after himself.","voice":null,"roles":["S","V","M","S","V","V","O","O","O","O","O","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"Today, millions of software developers have used Git to manage their daily work.","voice":null,"roles":["M","S","S","S","S","V","V","O","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null}],"bands":{"core1000":78,"core1800":13,"ngsl":1,"tsl":5,"nawl":0,"beyond":3},"grammar":[{"i":0,"focus":"which is a tool that tracks code changes","title":"Stacked Relative Clauses","note":"This sentence connects two relative clauses using 'which' and 'that'. The pronoun 'which' provides extra descriptive context about Git, while 'that' specifies the function of the tool."},{"i":6,"focus":"have used Git","title":"Present Perfect Tense","note":"The present perfect tense 'have used' connects an action originating in the past with current status. It emphasizes the ongoing relevance and accumulated experience of developers using Git today."}]},"t4":{"sentences":[{"text":"In April 2005, programmer Linus Torvalds began designing Git, a version control system for tracking software updates.","voice":null,"roles":["M","M","M","S","S","S","V","O","O","O","O","O","O","O","O","O","O"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"Version control software allows large teams of developers to collaborate effectively on complex digital projects.","voice":null,"roles":["S","S","S","V","O","O","O","O","C","C","C","C","C","C","C"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"Torvalds urgently needed a modern tool to manage the underlying code for the Linux operating system.","voice":null,"roles":["S","M","V","O","O","O","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"Although the new program functioned well, its peculiar title generated widespread confusion among technology workers.","voice":null,"roles":["M","S","S","S","V","M","S","S","S","V","O","O","M","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"In British English vernacular, the word git is commonly used as a mild insult for an annoying individual.","voice":null,"roles":["M","M","M","M","S","S","S","V","V","V","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"When questioned about his unusual decision, Torvalds humorously reported that he names all his projects after himself.","voice":null,"roles":["M","M","M","M","M","M","S","M","V","M","S","V","O","O","O","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"He lightheartedly implied that his own personality matched the description of the British insult.","voice":null,"roles":["S","M","V","M","S","S","S","V","O","O","O","O","O","O"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null},{"text":"Today, Git is widely recognized as the standard version control tool relied upon by software engineers everywhere.","voice":null,"roles":["M","S","V","V","V","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":null,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null}],"bands":{"core1000":67,"core1800":13,"ngsl":6,"tsl":5,"nawl":1,"beyond":8},"grammar":[{"i":1,"focus":"allows large teams of developers to collaborate","title":"Verb + Object + Infinitive","note":"The structure 'allow + object + to-infinitive' indicates enabling someone to perform an action. Here, 'large teams of developers' is the object doing the action expressed by 'to collaborate'."},{"i":3,"focus":"Although the new program functioned well","title":"Concession Clause with Although","note":"The conjunction 'although' introduces a subordinate clause expressing contrast. It sets up an opposing fact before presenting the main clause outcome."}]},"t5":{"sentences":[{"text":"In April 2005, software engineer Linus Torvalds set out to construct Git, a distributed version control system for tracking source code changes.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t5-0.m4a","roles":["M","M","M","S","S","S","S","V","V","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":10.1,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.2,0.6,1.92,2.26,2.96,3.58,4.32,4.6,4.78,4.92,5.26,6.28,6.44,6.78,7.16,7.56,8.12,8.42,8.72,9.08,9.38]},{"text":"Having previously created the Linux operating system kernel, Torvalds required an exceptionally fast tool to coordinate thousands of parallel development tasks.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t5-1.m4a","roles":["M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","S","V","O","O","O","O","M","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":9.64,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.18,0.56,1.26,1.48,1.72,2.22,2.76,3.66,4.1,4.38,4.76,5.04,5.82,6.32,6.6,6.94,7.6,8.0,8.24,8.94]},{"text":"Existing options failed to satisfy his demanding criteria, prompting him to write an entirely new system in less than two weeks.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t5-2.m4a","roles":["S","S","V","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":7.86,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.3,0.58,1.06,1.34,1.82,2.06,2.46,3.72,3.9,4.18,4.32,4.52,4.72,5.16,5.64,6.04,6.4,6.6,6.86,7.12]},{"text":"While the resulting software revolutionized collaborative programming, its peculiar name generated considerable curiosity across the global software development community.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t5-3.m4a","roles":["M","S","S","S","V","O","O","S","S","S","V","O","O","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":9.43,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.12,0.24,0.52,1.14,2.3,2.64,3.84,3.94,4.32,4.92,5.48,6.0,6.7,7.36,7.6,7.8,8.2,8.62]},{"text":"In British English vernacular, the noun git serves as a colorful insult referring to an unpleasant or obnoxious person.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t5-4.m4a","roles":["M","M","M","M","S","S","S","V","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":7.39,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.1,0.4,0.68,1.8,1.9,2.04,2.6,3.08,3.42,3.5,3.82,4.48,4.86,5.2,5.32,5.82,6.28,6.64]},{"text":"When pressed on why he selected such a derogatory term, Torvalds responded with his characteristic self-deprecating humor.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t5-5.m4a","roles":["M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","M","S","V","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":6.96,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.12,0.36,0.64,0.92,1.14,1.46,1.84,1.96,2.22,3.34,3.78,4.08,4.42,4.56,5.4,6.36]},{"text":"He famously quipped that he was an egotistical creator who habitually named all of his software projects after himself.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t5-6.m4a","roles":["S","M","V","M","S","V","C","C","C","S","M","V","O","O","O","O","O","M","M"],"dur":6.12,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.06,0.32,0.86,1.0,1.16,1.3,1.4,2.06,2.54,2.9,3.44,3.78,4.14,4.3,4.44,4.68,5.2,5.56]},{"text":"Nearly two decades later, Git has become the indispensable backbone of modern software engineering, powering major repository platforms worldwide.","voice":"voice/reserve-code-why-linus-torvalds-named/t5-7.m4a","roles":["M","M","M","M","S","V","V","C","C","C","C","C","C","C","M","M","M","M","M"],"dur":9.14,"voiceJa":null,"durJa":null,"times":[0.0,0.16,0.52,0.86,1.64,1.84,2.06,2.28,2.56,3.04,3.8,4.08,4.32,4.8,5.78,6.14,6.54,6.92,7.66]}],"bands":{"core1000":63,"core1800":14,"ngsl":5,"tsl":5,"nawl":0,"beyond":13},"grammar":[{"i":1,"focus":"Having previously created the Linux operating system kernel","title":"Perfect Participle Clause","note":"A perfect participle clause ('Having + past participle') modifies the main clause subject and highlights an action completed before the main verb took place. Here, it concisely gives background rationale for why Torvalds needed a fast tool."},{"i":2,"focus":"prompting him to write an entirely new system","title":"Present Participle Result Clause","note":"An -ing participle clause at the end of a sentence often expresses the result or consequence of the main clause action. It connects cause and effect smoothly without starting a new sentence."}]}},"glossary":[{"word":"slang","lemma":"slang","pos":"noun","ipa":"/slæŋ/","definition":"Informal words used by a specific group of people.","tiers":["t2","t3"]},{"word":"collaborate","lemma":"collaborate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kəˈlæbəˌreɪt/","definition":"To work together with others on a shared project.","tiers":["t4"]},{"word":"insult","lemma":"insult","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪnsʌlt/","definition":"An offensive or disrespectful remark aimed at someone.","tiers":["t4","t5"]},{"word":"peculiar","lemma":"peculiar","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/pɪˈkjuːljər/","definition":"Strange, unusual, or unexpected in a surprising way.","tiers":["t4","t5"]},{"word":"vernacular","lemma":"vernacular","pos":"noun","ipa":"/vərˈnækjələr/","definition":"The informal language spoken by people in a specific region.","tiers":["t4","t5"]},{"word":"quipped","lemma":"quip","p